Maintainer risk: MEDIUM — three real correctness/security issues (P0 wire leak via From<BackendChangesError> bypassing the bd:JMAP-wlip.2 redaction; civil_from_days panic on adversarial clock; createdIds collision semantics undocumented) plus three P1 maintainer-burden items. Cancellation gap (lib.rs:185-227) and CallerCtx clone cost are both documented but not in README gotchas. None of the issues are critical to landing the bench/example/docs work — but JMAP-jfia.1 is a real defence-in-depth gap that the e9e760c bd:JMAP-wlip.2 closeout missed.
Confidence: ~85% chance of no missed correctness bug; ~70% for missed security gap. Least confident flags: JMAP-jfia.2 (civil_from_days panic) — the exact boundary between the i64-secs sentinel and the i32-year overflow needs a numeric probe to confirm the gap is reachable on real SystemTime impls, not just theoretical. JMAP-jfia.3 (createdIds collision) — the RFC 8620 §3.4 wording does not clearly mandate either behaviour; the call to file as P0 vs P1 is judgment. Uncertainty driven by: (1) only static review, no behavioural probing — read-only review pass per /review-myoss rules forbids running test scripts that aren't already in the tree; (2) didn't audit the 30+ commits worth of doc changes for inconsistencies (only the source diffs got the full read); (3) didn't trace the resolve_args + optional_arg interaction beyond the one filed example (JMAP-jfia.15). Would raise confidence if: a behavioural probe confirmed JMAP-jfia.2 reachability, the workspace had a fuzz harness for parse_request + resolve_args + dispatch, and a wire-format conformance test suite (like Stalwart's jmap-test-suite) ran against the dispatcher.
API contract risk: MEDIUM — 13 api-contract findings filed (.17–.29) covering the canonical-template foundation crate that every *-server extension cookie-cuts from. Two P1 immutability items (SetError.extra pub field bypasses with_extra reserved-name guard at .17; HandlerFuture anonymous tuple return locks out future fields at .19) and five P2 items spanning typed-error contract enforcement (.20), parse-time depth-cap unenforceability (.21), pub-const-locks-consumers (.18), broken UTCDate newtype invariant (.23), and 8-arg positional query signatures that force breaks on every new RFC param (.26). The kit posture (library, not server) is correct — the issues are the cost of NOT planning the additive-evolution shape before each surface ossifies. None of the api-contract items are wire-format security bugs by themselves; the security risk in JMAP-jfia.1 / .2 / .3 from the my-oss pass is unrelated to this supplement.
API contract confidence: ~75% chance of no missed contract issue. Least confident flags: (a) JMAP-jfia.20 (JmapBackend::Error Display doc-only) — the credential-leak class is real, but enumerating every consumer-side leak path (tracing::error!, structured logging, panic-payload formatters, metrics labels) without running the actual extension *-server crates is partial; a workspace-wide grep for 'tracing::.*?err' or '%err' against every consumer would raise confidence; (b) JMAP-jfia.26 (query_objects 8-arg) — the recommended struct refactor assumes the trait method shape stays positional-arg long enough to matter; a competing finding could argue the kit will move to per-extension backend traits before this hurts; (c) JMAP-jfia.27 (account_exists perf) — no probe to confirm extension *-server crates actually call it per-method; the read of handlers.rs:87-93 shows 4 call sites in this crate alone, but the extension crates could be batching. Uncertainty driven by: (1) no behavioural probing — supplement is read-only per /review-myoss rules; (2) didn't audit jmap-mail-server / jmap-chat-server (out of scope) to confirm the 8-arg query_objects signature is actually causing churn there; (3) didn't follow the workspace AGENTS.md extras-preservation pattern to check whether JmapResponse / Invocation already have an extras slot that could carry DispatchOutcome-style metadata losslessly (would change the .29 framing). Would raise confidence if: a workspace-wide cargo-public-api diff vs an older release showed how often these surfaces actually have evolved; if the upcoming major bump (mentioned in workspace AGENTS.md canonical-templates section) had a concrete cutoff date for landing additive-shape refactors.
Idiom rating: ACCEPTABLE — six Rust-idiom findings filed (.30–.35), two at P1 (sentinel-string return value at .30, magic-zero enum-variant overload at .31), three at P2 (builder API asymmetry at .32, per-call allocation in Serialize at .33, manual-strip clippy pattern at .34), one at P3 (defensive unwrap_or_else dead branch at .35). No P0 idiom-grade soundness issues. The crate reads as careful Rust: extras-preservation pattern is correctly implemented with serde flatten, #[non_exhaustive] is applied to every public struct/enum that needs it, #[forbid(unsafe_code)] is at the crate root, every helper has a #[cfg(test)] block with hand-derived oracles. The wincing-points are concentrated in three places: (a) failure-mode shape — sentinel-string returns and magic-zero variant overloads where Result/distinct variants belong; (b) per-call allocations in hot-path Serialize impls that collect_str would eliminate; (c) one builder-API asymmetry between &str and impl Into<String> on a single type. None require deep restructuring; all are mechanically safe edits. The crate is the canonical foundation for every *-server extension in the workspace, so propagation discipline matters more here than in a leaf crate.
Rust idiom confidence: ~80% chance of no missed idiom issue worth filing.   Least confident flags: JMAP-jfia.33 (collect_str perf claim) — the per-call allocation savings depend on the underlying Serializer's collect_str impl; serde_json's collect_str DOES use a stack buffer for short strings (verified in serde_json source at de.rs), but a future Serializer wired by a consumer could in theory be slower with collect_str. Marginal worry. JMAP-jfia.31 (magic-zero variant) — the design choice to overload TooManyChanges { limit: 0 } is consistent with how the wire layer treats cannotCalculateChanges as a degenerate TooManyChanges and the existing test backend_changes_error_limit_zero_maps_to_cannot_calculate documents the intent. A reasonable counter-argument is 'the magic-zero shape is the cost of fitting two RFC error variants into one backend-error envelope without exposing the wire-level distinction'. I judge two-variant cleaner but the call is judgment, not bug.   Uncertainty driven by: (1) no behavioural probing — read-only review per /review-rusty rules; couldn't run criterion benches to confirm the allocation cost of .33 is measurable; (2) didn't audit how the existing *-server extension crates (jmap-mail-server, jmap-chat-server) consume BackendChangesError::TooManyChanges to confirm whether they actually pattern-match on the magic-zero shape — that would change the migration cost estimate on .31; (3) didn't audit how downstream consumers of now_utc_string() handle the 'clock-out-of-range' sentinel — none in this crate but the workspace has eight other crates that re-export or call helpers from jmap-server. Would raise confidence if: (a) cargo clippy --workspace -W clippy::pedantic was run against the crate to flag the manual_strip / use_self / etc. lints automatically; (b) a microbench around SetErrorType::serialize was added to confirm the allocation cost in .33 is real (or not). Bikeshedding restraint: I stopped at 6 findings. The remaining read-through surfaced no idiom issue with a concrete what-goes-wrong story beyond what was already filed in the my-oss pass or the 6 above.
